# The bundle: the conventions that ship as RULES rather than as branches (§4).
#
# Every rule here is a name that used to be in Appendix C's census with an
# interpreter phase behind it, and is now data -- readable by R4's queries,
# defeasible by `overrides`, and preemptable because it is selected like
# anything else.
#
# This file used to be `Machine._install_bundle`, ~250 lines of
# `self.rules.rule(IMPLIES, [Member(PLUS, g.rel(...))], ...)`. Moving it here is
# not tidying. The design's claim is that the HOW is data; authoring the
# apparatus in Python meant nobody had ever checked that the SURFACE can say
# what the apparatus is made of. It could not: `arrived` and `not` were
# unnameable in a corpus, so <intake> and <denial> were unwritable by anyone but
# the engine. Nothing reported that -- it is the same twin-node trap the loader's
# own comment records for `says` and `overrides`, arriving from the vocabulary
# side instead of the minting side. (A third suspected gap, the `?` sign as an
# ARGUMENT, turned out not to block anything here; see `Machine.reserved`.)
#
# ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING. §18's tiebreak is authored order, and these are
# installed first, which reproduces the old behaviour where a phase ran before
# any rule was considered. That is a precedence CLAIM, and being a claim rather
# than a control-flow fact is the whole point: a corpus can override it. Two
# thirds of this agent's arbitrations are still settled by the order in this
# file, so it is worth reading as an argument and not as a list.
#
# What did NOT move, and stays machinery: the `standing` deposit for each rule
# below, the write-time hooks (`_dispatch`, `_enter`, `_fit`, `_settle`,
# `_verdict`, `_remember`) and the norm veto (`_forbid`). §21 already calls
# those honest debt.


# -- the boundary ----------------------------------------------------------

# What a report MEANS. Crossing the boundary stays machinery, because a channel
# is anchored and a rule is generic; deciding that an arrival is a saying does
# not, and never did.
rule <intake> = implies(
    { +arrived(?channel, ?said, ?sign) },
    { +says(?channel, ?said, ?sign) } )

# What an emission MEANS: the agent acted.
rule <did> = implies(
    { +emitted(?what) },
    { +did(?what) } )

# And the same, one row rather than one branch, for an act decided on inside a
# hypothesis. `did` under a supposition means *under this, I did* -- which is
# what the frame already means -- so <assert-act> supplies the assumed outcome
# and planning continues past the step.
rule <taken> = implies(
    { +taken(?what) },
    { +did(?what) } )

# §15's *the agent asserts the act*, which was an unarguable line of the
# interpreter and is now a defeasible claim. The consequent is a bare variable,
# which §12 calls vacuous BACKWARDS and exact forwards -- the same shape as a
# trust rule, and for the same reason: it says *believe what this channel
# reported*, where the channel is the agent's own hands.
#
# `no substituted(?what)` is how a corpus takes that back for one act rather
# than for all of them: a rule supplying an act's outcome concludes
# `substituted(?a)` as well, and this rule then assumes nothing about that act
# while every other act keeps the fallback. That is per ACT, where precedence
# is per rule and per tick.
rule <assert-act> = implies(
    { +did(?what), no substituted(?what) },
    { +?what } )


# -- denial ----------------------------------------------------------------

# Denial as a term and denial as a sign are the same claim, so a corpus must not
# have to know which one it is looking at. Crossing back into a supposition
# unwraps `likely(not(b))` to `not(b)`, and the rules inside are written
# against `-b`.
#
# One direction only, and the asymmetry is the point. `+not(?p)` is a claim the
# machinery may have manufactured while re-wrapping, so it is translated back.
# The reverse -- minting `+not(p)` for every denial in the graph -- would double
# every negative fact, and would build `not(not(p))` the moment it met its own
# output. §9 names that as the cost of wrappers; this is where the cost is
# declined.
rule <denial> = implies(
    { +not(?denied) },
    { -?denied } )


# -- surprise --------------------------------------------------------------

# §18 says it in one line -- *an expected entry and an observed entry that
# disagree* -- and as a phase that line was false of the implementation: the
# comparison was Python, so no rule could see it, reorder it, or refuse it.
#
# Four ROWS, not four branches -- and until this file they were four rows
# generated by a Python `for` loop over four tuples, which is a branch wearing a
# row's clothes. Two expected signs times the two ways an observation can
# contradict one: the opposite sign, and `?`. The last is the case a phase gets
# right by accident and a rule has to state -- §9's `?` invalidates without
# replacing, so *I can no longer say* is a disappointed expectation exactly as
# much as *the opposite happened*.
#
# The second member is a bare variable, already bound by the first, so it matches
# the observation of that very proposition under the sign that disappoints it.
rule <deviation-+-contradicted> = implies(
    { +expects(?p, plus), -?p },
    { +deviates(?p) } )

rule <deviation-+-invalidated> = implies(
    { +expects(?p, plus), ? ?p },
    { +deviates(?p) } )

rule <deviation---contradicted> = implies(
    { +expects(?p, minus), +?p },
    { +deviates(?p) } )

rule <deviation---invalidated> = implies(
    { +expects(?p, minus), ? ?p },
    { +deviates(?p) } )


# -- backward reading, entire ----------------------------------------------
#
# The last phase, as six rules over three requests (`ugm.backward` measured these
# against it, one rule deleted at a time, before it went).
#
# §14 prints a rule for this and it cannot work: `con(?r, ?pat, +)` binds the
# rule's stored PATTERN, which is generic, and a goal is ground, so one variable
# cannot bind to both. Deciding they correspond is `match`, and match is floor.
# The repair was not to make backward reading a primitive but to give the wall a
# door: `fit` answers *could this rule produce this goal*, already instantiated,
# because a rule cannot hold a binding.
#
# What the phase was doing that these are not: ordering itself ahead of every
# rule and returning early. That was never backward reading -- it was a
# precedence claim in control flow, and it starved forward reasoning badly enough
# that goals the corpus could satisfy read as blocked.

# Ask what could produce this goal. Two rules, not one, and the split is the
# point: asking recall first means the reader asks about what came to mind,
# rather than about everything it has ever been told. That is §14's index -- 711
# applications of <ask-fit> became 8, one per goal.
rule <ask-recall> = implies(
    { +goal(?wanted) },
    { +recall(?wanted) } )

rule <ask-fit> = implies(
    { +recalled(?r, ?wanted) },
    { +fit(?r, ?wanted) } )

# A rule that fits is a plan. R7 for the search's own working state: the plan
# node is built by substitution, and substitution interns, so the same rule
# expanding the same goal names the same plan -- which is what a plan is. Minting
# a fresh node would be wrong, not merely wasteful.
rule <plan> = implies(
    { +fits(?r, ?wanted) },
    { +expands(plan(?r, ?wanted), ?wanted, ?r) } )

# What it needs becomes a subgoal of that plan. `?sub` arrives already
# instantiated, which is the whole reason `fit` answers with `need`.
rule <expand> = implies(
    { +fits(?r, ?wanted), +need(?r, ?wanted, ?sub) },
    { +subgoal(plan(?r, ?wanted), ?sub), +goal(?sub) } )

# Is it already satisfied? Asked inside the PLAN's bindings, so siblings agree
# about which tap (§18).
rule <ask-check> = implies(
    { +subgoal(plan(?r, ?wanted), ?sub) },
    { +check(plan(?r, ?wanted), ?sub) } )


# -- relevance -------------------------------------------------------------

# Which the engine was already computing and throwing away.
#
# `fits(<R>, w)` says *this rule could produce what you want* -- it is the answer
# to a match request the backward reader made, and it is exactly the claim a
# preference wants.
#
# ⚠⚠⚠ **`<relevant>` USED TO TRANSLATE IT INTO A SCORE, and that rule is gone
# with `prefer`.** It read `fits(?r, ?wanted)` and concluded
# `prefer(?r, ?wanted, 1)` -- means-ends analysis as a rule-keyed buff. The
# KNOWLEDGE is untouched: the backward reader still works out which rule serves
# the goal and still says so, in `fits`. What went is only the translation into
# a number attached to a rule id, which is what could not survive a rule being
# adopted, composed or renamed.
#
# ⚠ Nothing replaced it, because nothing could: attention names a NODE and
# `fits` names a RULE, so *prefer the rules serving my goal* is not a sentence
# attention can say. That is a real loss of a bundled behaviour and it is
# recorded rather than papered over -- an agent wanting it back writes the rule
# above into its own corpus.


# -- the verdict -----------------------------------------------------------

# Asked when the loop has stopped, which is the only moment at which *nothing
# fits this* is a claim about a finished search rather than about how far one has
# got. This is the policy the phase asserted in control flow; here it is one
# rule, and a corpus that wants to give up earlier or later overrides it.
rule <give-up> = implies(
    { +quiet(?m), +goal(?wanted) },
    { +verdict(?wanted) } )


# ...and no sibling for the veto's occasion, which is worth a note because one
# was written and deleted. `+open(?w) => +verdict(?w)` looked exactly parallel and
# `ugm.bundle` reported it blind; deleting it broke no check, and the reason is
# the veto's own shape. Because an outstanding goal *outranks* an `enough` rather
# than delaying it, a run with an open goal always finishes at quiescence -- so
# `quiet` is always written, and <give-up> above already asks the verdict for
# every goal. The occasion is not redundant (it says *which goal was nearly
# dropped*, which nothing else records); a second way to ask about it was.
#
# What is deliberately not bundled either is the REACTION.
# `+open(?w), +blocked(?w) => +doing(ask(?w))` is one line and a corpus writes it,
# because where a question goes is a fact about a deployment and not about
# reasoning. What ships is the occasion; §19 argues the split.


# -- what one locus can see of another (§11) ---------------------------------
#
# Three of the four directions used to be a Python branch inside
# `Moment.at_or_after`, which is the read's own containment test. Two of them
# are here now and the third is the absence of a rule.
#
# The machinery consults these ON DEMAND, with both loci already bound, the way
# `_forbid` consults `forbidden` and `precedence()` reads what the graph claims.
# It is not a fixpoint and nothing has to be selected: a rule that must win a
# move before the read can answer would make a span claim invisible until it
# did, which is the failure this design refuses.
#
# ⚠ A claim about an INSTANT is not a claim about a stretch, so nothing here
# concludes `reaches(<span>, <moment>)`. That is the load-bearing one: letting a
# moment's claim inherit into a span would answer *did it hold throughout* from
# an entry that says only *it held then*, and a denial in the middle of the
# stretch is exactly what `resolve` cannot see. Written as a missing rule rather
# than as a returned False, so a corpus that disagrees adds a row.
# ⚠⚠⚠ **The span rules went with the locus.** `<span-complete>` and
# `<span-itself>` concluded `reaches(?m, ?s)`, which was the policy `Moment
# .at_or_after` consulted to decide whether a claim ABOUT A STRETCH was visible
# from a moment. An entry has no locus now, so nothing can be about a stretch
# and there is nothing for the policy to decide. §11's recognitions over a
# stretch are still sayable -- `span(?a, ?b)` is an ordinary node and a corpus
# can conclude whatever it likes about one -- but the claim is deposited now,
# like every other claim, rather than dated to the stretch.


# -- the call stack (§18) ----------------------------------------------------
#
# The plumbing under a recursive plan, and deliberately NOT a way of making one.
# `<expand>` above is a STRATEGY -- means-ends, decompose a goal by some rule's
# antecedents -- and it is one strategy among several. These three rules are
# what any strategy needs underneath it: spawn a sub-call, wait, propagate the
# return.
#
# ⭐⭐⭐ **A call carries its parameters as ONE node**, which is the whole of what
# makes this parametric. `call(?c, tower(?d, ?f, ?t, ?s))` puts the arity in the
# domain's hands, so nothing here knows how many parameters a plan has or what
# they mean. Exercised by two domains that share nothing else -- `ugm.hanoi` and
# the countdown beside it.
#
# ⚠ The stage ORDER is data, not rules: `advances(?p, ?q)` and `closes(?p)` are
# facts a corpus deposits, because what differs between one recursive plan and
# the next is exactly the order of its steps. The bundle supplies the stack; the
# corpus supplies the strategy.
#
# ⚠⚠ And a stage cannot be read off the WORLD, which is why this exists at all.
# `ugm.hanoi` records four corpora that failed first, and the fourth is the
# argument: Hanoi's recursion is depth-first and ordered, and `at(d1, x)` is
# equally true on the way out and on the way back. A guard read off the state is
# ambiguous by construction. The stage is the disambiguation, and it has to be a
# fact of its own.

# One node per application, so two calls with the same parameters are two calls.
# That is not a nicety: `solve(d1, x, z, y)` occurs TWICE in a three-disk Hanoi,
# and a call keyed on its arguments would collide with itself.
rule <call-spawn> = implies( { +spawn(?c, ?args, ?stage) },
                        { +call(+k, ?args), +stage(+k, ?stage),
                          +awaits(?c, +k), -spawn(?c, ?args, ?stage) } )

# The child returned, and this call has more to do.
rule <call-advance> = implies( { +stage(?c, ?p), +awaits(?c, ?k), +returned(?k),
                            +advances(?p, ?q) },
                          { -stage(?c, ?p), +stage(?c, ?q), -awaits(?c, ?k) } )

# ...or it does not, and the call returns in its turn.
rule <call-return> = implies( { +stage(?c, ?p), +awaits(?c, ?k), +returned(?k),
                           +closes(?p) },
                         { -stage(?c, ?p), +returned(?c) } )
